#!/bin/sh
# A stand-in for xtb, so a test can be SUPPLIED the binary instead of
# skipped when the machine has none.
#
# Measured before this was written (2026-10-05), over the 37 test files
# that gate on xtb: 904 tests collected, 782 pass with no xtb and 122
# skip. With a stub that exists and computes NOTHING, 892 pass -- so 110
# of those 122 never consumed xtb output at all. They gate on its
# presence and then test a command line, a parse, a guard or an error
# path. Five tests in four files genuinely need output; they keep their
# skip, because a recorded energy replayed back is not a test of the
# physics it claims to check.
#
# So this stub answers the two things presence-gated code asks -- "are
# you there" and "which version" -- and refuses everything else loudly
# rather than inventing a result. A silent zero exit with empty output
# would make a parse return nothing and the failure land three layers
# away from the cause.
case "$*" in
    *--version*)
        echo "xtb version 6.7.1 compatible (DELFIN test stand-in)"
        exit 0
        ;;
esac

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
DELFIN test stand-in for xtb: no output is recorded for this invocation.

This stub exists so tests that only need xtb to BE THERE can run on a
machine without it. A test that needs a real calculation must either
keep its skip, or carry a recorded output as a fixture -- see
tests/test_a_test_is_bound_to_delfin_not_to_a_machine.py.
MSG
echo "invocation: $*" >&2
exit 1
